  "messages": [
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Choose one next planning step using the local decision below. Return exactly one listed statement and stop. No other planning choices have been supplied.\n{\"context\": \"Contributors can use several optional arrival windows, and a small walk-in allowance remains available throughout intake.\", \"decision\": \"How should contribution arrivals be spread across the intake period?\", \"goal\": \"Help usable household goods reach interested neighbors while keeping contributions, claims, and final collection understandable.\", \"problem\": \"Neighbors are organizing a one-day fair for clean, usable household goods that people no longer need. Giving and taking are both optional, and no payment or matching contribution is required. Volunteers have intake space, category tables, reserve shelves, a holding area, carts, and simple item records. Contributors choose in advance whether unclaimed goods should be returned during the closing collection period or donated through an arranged pickup.\"}\nCANDIDATE STATEMENTS:\nContributors choose available arrival windows on the intake sheet before bringing their household goods. People organize their own visits within visible capacity, giving volunteers a rough forecast of when incoming contributions will arrive.\nContributors receive staggered arrival suggestions by neighborhood block and may choose another open window when needed. Nearby households get a simple shared plan while individual conflicts can be accommodated within the remaining intake capacity.\nOrganizers assign suggested arrival windows after contributors report their availability, keeping the walk-in allowance open. The intake load can be spread across the period while taking account of when contributors can realistically attend."
    }
  ],
